Credits

No third-party code is included. How the TV's local API (SSAP, a WebSocket on
ports 3001 and 3000) works, and the form of the registration manifest, were
learned from LG's Connect SDK (https://github.com/ConnectSDK, Apache License 2.0),
Home Assistant's aiowebostv (https://github.com/home-assistant-libs/aiowebostv,
Apache License 2.0), LGWebOSRemote (https://github.com/klattimer/LGWebOSRemote,
MIT License), lgtv2 (https://github.com/hobbyquaker/lgtv2, MIT License) and
openHAB's LG webOS binding (https://github.com/openhab/openhab-addons, Eclipse
Public License 2.0), and from their issue reports about LG's 2026 firmware. No
code was copied from them.

The WebSocket client follows the design of DirectorLink's own
(driver/src/cloud/websocket.lua, https://github.directorlink.io, Apache License
2.0). Finding TVs (SSDP) follows Snap One's SSDP module as DirectorLink uses it.
Control4 interfaces follow the public Snap One DriverWorks documentation
(https://snap-one.github.io/docs-driverworks-fundamentals/).

The test fixtures in tests/ follow the shape of the TV's messages as those
clients record them and describe a made-up home; names, addresses, keys and ids
in them are invented.
